================================================================
SCENE FRAMING
================================================================

The three positions together depict a single battle scene: the LEFT creature and the RIGHT creature are facing each other as opponents, with the CENTER pose as a hero presentation between them. This framing drives the pose geometry below.

POSES (left → center → right, same creature throughout)
All three poses must express the creature's BATTLE ROLE. Read the role's STANCE, LIMBS, ENERGY, and SILHOUETTE descriptors above and bake them into every pose — a Glass Cannon's combat stance differs fundamentally from a Wall's combat stance, even at the same camera angle. Tier dictates how much detail; role dictates how the body is held.

1. LEFT — Full back view, expressing the role from behind.
   The camera is positioned behind the creature. Only the back, rear, tail, and backs of the ears/wings/limbs are visible. The face is NOT visible — at most a tiny sliver of cheek or the back of the head. Body oriented toward the right side of the canvas, as if squaring up against the creature in the right position. Body posture must convey the role's energy from behind: a Wall stands square and rooted from behind; a Glass Cannon's back is coiled and ready to spring; a Cleric's back is open and unguarded; a Wallbreaker's shoulders are loaded and bunched. Think: a trainer's view of their own Pokémon from behind during a battle.

2. CENTER — Hero showcase pose, facing the viewer, expressing the role front-on.
   Official Pokémon artwork style — the creature's flagship presentation image. Facing the camera directly (or at most a subtle 5-10° turn), full body visible. Expression and gesture must read the BATTLE ROLE clearly: a Sweeper smirks confidently mid-stride, a Wall stands immovable with arms crossed, a Cleric extends open palms, a Glass Cannon coils with manic intensity, a Staller stands serene and unhurried. The signature gesture should communicate role + typing simultaneously. NOT a neutral at-rest pose — this is the creature's "cover of the box" moment.

3. RIGHT — Combat stance specific to the role's archetypal attack form.
   The exact stance is dictated by the role's STANCE and LIMBS descriptors — NOT a generic "aggressive ready" pose. A Wallbreaker winds up a heavy strike; a Sweeper is mid-stride extending forward; a Wall plants and braces in defense; a Staller barely shifts weight; a Glass Cannon is mid-pounce; a Cleric raises arms to channel energy. Body angled three-quarter toward the viewer, facing the LEFT pose's back. Intensity calibrated to the design tier — restrained for runt, commanding for apex, reality-bending for mythic.

Adapt all pose language to the creature's actual anatomy. If it has no arms, no wings, or unusual anatomy (serpentine, blob, quadruped, floating), interpret "limbs raised," "signature gesture," "arms crossed," or "squared up" accordingly. Do NOT add wings, arms, or features that aren't part of the established design.